      "text": "```python\n\"\"\"\nLeast Recently Used (LRU) Cache Implementation\n\nAn LRU cache is a fixed-size cache that evicts the least recently used items\nwhen the cache is full. This implementation uses a combination of a doubly\nlinked list and a hash map to achieve O(1) time complexity for both get and\nput operations.\n\nHow it works:\n- The doubly linked list maintains the order of usage: most recently used at\n  the head, least recently used at the tail.\n- The hash map provides O(1) lookup of nodes in the linked list.\n- On a 'get' or 'put' operation, the accessed node is moved to the head of\n  the list (marking it as most recently used).\n- When the cache is full and a new item is inserted, the node at the tail\n  (least recently used) is evicted.\n\nTime Complexity:\n- get(key): O(1) average case\n- put(key, value): O(1) average case\n- Space Complexity: O(capacity)\n\nExample:\n    >>> cache = LRUCache(2)\n    >>> cache.put(1, 1)\n    >>> cache.put(2,",
